    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The largest Andean state before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>, ruling about 1,000 km of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s desert coast from its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>’s walled royal compounds held palaces, storerooms and burial platforms; each new king seems to have built his own.</li><li>Irrigation, conquest and skilled crafts made it rich; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered it around 1470 and took its metalworkers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Around 1450 about 140 children and 200 young llamas were sacrificed in a single event at Huanchaquito, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>, apparently soon after heavy rains and flooding.</p></aside>
    </section>

